{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION of the confirmed root cause — from a different agent, different ticket\n\nStory `divine-streaky-betafish` (epic 6202 S3, journald bounding, Gerrit change 2643) measured the\nhost's **actual retained journal at 1.7 GB**, while working a separate ticket with no knowledge of\nthis investigation's threshold.\n\n**That number lands directly in the confirmed overrun band.**\n\nThe sandbox experiment for this ticket established, under gp3-throttled conditions (3,000 IOPS /\n125 MB/s, 16 KiB granularity):\n\n- **~1.37 GB is the threshold at which 12 unbounded scans exhaust the 300-second timer period.**\n- 1.86 GB measured at **407.5 s = 136% of the period**.\n\nThe real journal was **1.7 GB** — above the threshold, between the two measured points, in the\nregion where the run cannot complete within its period.\n\nThis is the strongest available substitute for the on-box evidence that the stop/start destroyed.\nIt was gathered for an unrelated purpose (sizing an explicit `SystemMaxUse` cap), by an agent that\nwas not told the threshold, so it is not confirmation-shaped reasoning. Independent measurement of\nthe causal variable, landing in the predicted range.\n\n## It also corrects a premise I was about to rely on\n\nS3's investigation found journald was **already capped**: `SystemMaxUse` defaults to\n`min(10% of filesystem, 4 GiB)` = 4 GiB here, and 1.7 GB is comfortably inside it. So the framing\n\"the journal is unbounded\" — which appears in this ticket's earlier analysis — is **wrong**. The\njournal was within its implicit ceiling the whole time.\n\nThat matters, because it sharpens what the defect actually is. The defect is NOT \"an unbounded\njournal grew until scans became slow\". It is that **12 unbounded SCANS of a perfectly\nnormal-sized, correctly-capped journal cannot complete in the period once the page cache is cold.**\nThe journal did nothing wrong. No journald cap would have prevented this outage — a 3 GiB cap\nstill leaves 3 GiB to scan twelve times.\n\nThe fix therefore has to be bounding the SCANS and giving the unit a start timeout. Capping the\njournal is worth doing (S3's own reasons: a ceiling that does not move on volume resize, readable\non the box, named generator) but it is **not a fix for this defect**, and should not be recorded\nas one.\n\n## Second corroboration: the metric-zeroing hazard\n\nS3's plan reviewer flagged that restarting journald without the fd store would sever running\nservices' stdout streams and **silently zero the marker-count metrics `observability.sh` derives\nfrom those journals** — healthy readings from a box that had stopped reporting.\n\nThat is the same inversion this ticket documents from the other direction: the probe's contract is\nthat absence means the host is dead, and here a plausible ZERO would mean the same thing while\nreading as health. Two independent routes to \"the observability path can lie about the thing it\nobserves\" is a stronger signal about that path than either alone.\n\nS3 verified the fd store is present (`FileDescriptorStoreMax=4224`,\n`FileDescriptorStorePreserve=yes`) and made its restart refuse when it is absent.\n\n## Effect on the outstanding on-box queries\n\nThe four bounded read-only `journalctl` queries remain useful but are **no longer load-bearing for\nthe mechanism**. What they would still add: direct proof that THIS run latched (a\n`Starting rebar health…` with no matching `Finished`), and the CURRENT journal size — which\ndetermines whether the host is above the ~1.37 GB threshold right now and therefore whether this\nrecurs before the fix lands. The 1.7 GB figure suggests it is."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788564205609996001,"uuid":"beaf513d-1587-4811-a2cf-e0718d2a5050"}